reasoning:
The user wants: when hovering, the `.info` div should appear behind the card (using backdrop/filter or position), and the image should become blurry. They want a 3D effect where the info goes to the back face.

Approaches:
1. Using `backface-visibility: hidden` on the card so the back face is not visible normally, then reveal it on hover with `transform: rotateY(180deg)` on the card — but they also want the image to blur.
2. Use CSS `backdrop-filter: blur()` on the card — but backdrop filter only works if there's something behind it... Actually you can set backdrop-filter on any element that has an overflow beyond its boundaries? No, backdrop-filter blurs what's behind the element in the z-index layering context — it needs contents overflowing or positioned behind. But actually, backdrop-filter applies to background too? It filters compositing of layers including backgrounds and children below? Let me think.

Backdrop filter operates on the compositing layers between the element and the document root. So if you have something behind the element (e.g., another card or the body content), the backdrop filter will blur it. But in their case, nothing is behind the cards presumably, so it might just blur the background color. Actually backdrop-filter does work on solid backgrounds in some browsers — it blurs the area behind the box including its background, essentially acting like a vignette + blur on the background. Hmm, actually it works: a blurred background effect is commonly done with backdrop-filter: blur() on an element. But it requires... no special requirements; it will blur whatever is behind it including the page content behind and the element's own background. Yes, this works fine.

But they want the `.info` to appear "à l'arrière" — behind the image. A classic approach: use `perspective` + transform, or flip the card with `rotateY` and hide the front face with `backface-visibility: hidden`.

Better: Make the card itself do a Y rotation on hover, and use `backface-visibility: hidden` so the "other side" (behind) shows. But the info is inside the card front face; to show it on the back, we'd need to reposition it or duplicate it on the back side via transform: translateZ(-50px)? That gets complicated because flipping text would be mirrored.

Alternative simpler approach: On hover, apply `filter: blur()` to the img, and move the .info behind using `position: absolute; inset: 0; ... z-index: -1`? No — that would place it behind children but still within the card's painting context, so it wouldn't be visible through the image unless we use `mix-blend-mode: multiply` on the image! That's neat: keep the image in place, set it to mix-blend-mode: normal... Actually trick: put .info behind the image with position relative to the image or the card, z-index: -1 relative to image, and set image to `mix-blend-mode: multiply` (or overlay) so the info becomes visible through it. Plus blur the image for subtlety.

Or even simpler and very common: 
```css
.carte {
  position: relative;
  ...
  transition: transform 0.4s ease, filter 0.3s ease;
}
.carte:hover img {
  filter: blur(4px);
}
.carte:hover .info {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: var(--secondary-color); /* same bg */
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  z-index: -1; /* behind image if image is absolute-ish */
}
```
Wait, z-index: -1 on .info makes it behind other absolutely-positioned elements but still painted above non-local flow content? Position: absolute with z-index -1 places it behind inline-block/absolute siblings but painted above stacking context roots with higher z-index among same stacking context. The image isn't absolute here. Hmm, position static positioning: an element with position:static paints after its containing block's inline flow children... Actually the order matters: .carte contains img and .info. If .info is position: absolute z-index: -1, it becomes part of the card's stacking context and paints before other descendants of .carte (including img). But the issue: does it paint behind the image visually? Yes — same stacking context, lower z-index = painted earlier. And the image is in the same stacking context (block-level sibling). So it would be behind the image. Then we set mix-blend-mode: multiply on img so the information text shows through slightly. But actually if we want full clarity, maybe make .info have a semi-transparent background or white text. Also, we could avoid mix-blend-mode complexity by making .info fill the whole card and show through with blend mode, while keeping the image... hmm but then image would be occluded unless blend mode.

Alternatively, the cleaner "flip" approach:
```css
.carte {
  perspective: 1000px;
  transform-style: preserve-gradients;
  transition: transform 0.4s ease;
}
.carte:hover {
  transform: rotateY(-20deg);
}
.carte .info {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
  background: var(--secondary-color);
  transform: translateZ(-100px); /* moves to back face */
  ...hidden initially? 
}
```
Hmm, translateZ moves in 3D space but it doesn't flip; the element stays on its "front" face but displaced back. With perspective, you see it receding. To truly go behind, combine with rotateY and backface-visibility.

Actually, the most elegant: Make the card rotate, and make .info have `backface-visibility: hidden` on the card (so front face hidden when rotated), and create the info as being positioned on the back side. But mirroring text (rotateY flips horizontal text) — we can counteract with `transform: scaleX(-1)` on .info? When rotating the card around Y axis by ~180°, text mirrors horizontally. To fix: `.info { transform: scaleX(-1); }` but then on hover it's also scaled by parent... Actually if we set transform on .info relative to card's transform chain, we can compensate. This is getting complex.

Maybe simplest robust solution that meets the request: 
- Card has `position: relative;`
- Image blurs on hover with `filter: blur()` (with transition).
- `.info` with `position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1;` and appropriate padding so it covers the card; style it nicely (maybe dark background or white text since image blurred). Add `pointer-events: none`.

But wait — will .info paint behind the image given current layout? Both are block-level children of .carte. Block-level children: img flows first (top), then .info after it (below). If .info has position: absolute, it's taken out of normal flow and placed in the card's stacking context at position 0 0, z-index -1 → painted before img (which has no explicit position but is in normal flow, painting after any absolutely positioned descendants? Let me verify the rule: In same stacking context, an element with explicit position paints after all block-level inline-flow descendants of its containing block that end up in the same stacking context? Actually the ordering: absolutely positioned elements paint after their containing block's flow children? No! Absolutely positioned elements in the same stacking context paint BEFORE other absolutely positioned elements with lower z-index, and how do they relate to non-absolute flow children? Flow children paint first, then absolutely positioned elements, ordered by z-index within that phase. Wait, I need to recall precisely.

CSS positioning spec: For painting in a stacking context:
